+/* The files SFTP has open, shared by all sessions.  z_fds_lock is a Zephyr
+ * mutex: wolfSSL's wc_LockMutex does nothing in a SINGLE_THREADED build, and
+ * sessions run on their own threads.
+ *
+ * FatFs here has no file locking (FF_FS_LOCK 0), and a FIL keeps the file's
+ * first cluster, size and directory entry.  A second FIL writing to a file, or
+ * a rename or unlink under an open writer, would cross-link clusters or write
+ * to a directory slot that has been reused; a reader whose clusters are freed
+ * would read another file's data.  So a file has at most one writer, and an
+ * operation that conflicts with an open file first closes it (z_park), which
+ * saves its data.  Afterwards the file is reopened where it now is (z_unpark),
+ * or, if it is gone or has been replaced, stays retired: the descriptor is
+ * reserved until the client closes it and any other use fails.  Refusing the
+ * operation instead would fail ordinary saves: sshfs sends CLOSE
+ * asynchronously, so "write, close, rename" can arrive with the rename first.
+ *
+ * This covers SFTP only.  The shell, the HTTP server and Python use the same
+ * files through fs_*, and names that reach one file by different spellings (an
+ * 8.3 alias, non-ASCII case) aren't recognised as the same file. */
